[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Binary Search Algorithm"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Binary search is an efficient algorithm for finding a target value within a sorted array. It works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. If the middle element is the target, the search is successful. If the target is less than the middle element, the search continues in the left half. If the target is greater than the middle element, the search continues in the right half. This process continues until the target is found or the interval is empty."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Steps"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's a step-by-step breakdown of the binary search algorithm:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Initialize 'low' to the index of the first element (0) and 'high' to the index of the last element (array.length - 1)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. While 'low' is less than or equal to 'high':"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Calculate the middle index: 'mid = low + (high - low) / 2'. This prevents potential integer overflow."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. If the element at 'mid' is equal to the target, return 'mid'."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"5. If the target is less than the element at 'mid', set 'high = mid - 1'."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"6. If the target is greater than the element at 'mid', set 'low = mid + 1'."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"7. If the loop finishes without finding the target, return -1 (or a suitable indicator that the target is not found)."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example (JavaScript)"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"function binarySearch(arr, target) {
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;

  while (low <= high) {
    let mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);

    if (arr[mid] === target) {
      return mid;
    } else if (target < arr[mid]) {
      high = mid - 1;
    } else {
      low = mid + 1;
    }
  }

  return -1;
}

// Example usage:
const sortedArray = [2, 5, 7, 8, 11, 12];
const targetValue = 13;
const index = binarySearch(sortedArray, targetValue);

if (index !== -1) {
  console.log('Target found at index: ' + index);
} else {
  console.log('Target not found in the array.');
}
"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Time and Space Complexity"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Binary search has a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the number of elements in the array. This is because the search interval is halved in each iteration. The space complexity is O(1) because it uses a constant amount of extra space, regardless of the input size."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Binary search requires the input array to be sorted. If the array is not sorted, it must be sorted first, which can be done using algorithms like merge sort or quicksort (with a time complexity of O(n log n))."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages and Disadvantages"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Advantages: Very efficient for searching in large, sorted datasets."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Disadvantages: Requires the data to be sorted, which adds overhead if the data is not already sorted. Not suitable for unsorted data or data that is frequently modified."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Mistakes"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Incorrectly updating 'low' or 'high' indices, leading to infinite loops or incorrect results."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not handling the case where the target is not found in the array."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integer overflow when calculating the middle index (use 'mid = low + (high - low) / 2' instead of 'mid = (low + high) / 2')."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Applications"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Binary search is widely used in various applications, including:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Searching for elements in sorted arrays and lists."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementing efficient lookup tables."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Finding the square root of a number."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Searching in databases and file systems."}]
